  "hints": [
    "Counting the nodes first takes two passes. Can two pointers moving at different speeds do it in one?",
    "Move slow one step and fast two steps at a time.",
    "Stop when fast is null or fast.next is null; slow is then at the (second) middle."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function middleValue(head) {\n  let slow = head\n  let fast = head\n  while (fast !== null && fast.next !== null) {\n    slow = slow.next\n    fast = fast.next.next\n  }\n  return slow.val\n}\n",
    "py": "def middle_value(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast is not None and fast[\"next\"] is not None:\n        slow = slow[\"next\"]\n        fast = fast[\"next\"][\"next\"]\n    return slow[\"val\"]\n",
    "explanation": "Fast covers two nodes for each node slow covers, so when fast runs off the end slow has covered half the list. The loop condition decides which middle you get for even lengths: this version stops on the second."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)",
    "notes": "The same fast/slow idea detects cycles (Floyd's algorithm) and finds the split point for merge sort on lists."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Change the loop so even-length lists return the first middle instead.",
    "How would you detect, and then locate the start of, a cycle?"
  ]
}
